#set page(width: 210mm, height: 297mm, margin: 12mm)
#set text(size: 8.5pt)
#columns(3, gutter: 8mm)[
  Typesetting is the composition of text by arranging physical type or
  digital equivalents. The stored letters and other symbols are retrieved
  and ordered according to a language's orthography for visual display.
  Typesetting requires the prior process of designing a font face.

  During much of the letterpress era, movable type was composed by hand.
  Each page was assembled letter by letter from cases of metal sorts, then
  locked into a forme before inking. The compositor worked from a printed
  or handwritten manuscript, reading backward text.

  COL-ONE-END marks the end of the first column here.

  COL-TWO-START opens the middle column of this three column page. Hot
  metal typesetting accelerated composition in the late nineteenth
  century. The Linotype cast whole lines as single slugs, and the Monotype
  machine cast individual characters under keyboard control. Both systems
  transformed newspaper and book production.

  Phototypesetting replaced metal with light-sensitive film in the mid
  twentieth century. Characters were projected through glass matrices
  onto photographic paper, allowing easy scaling and a far wider range of
  shapes than cast metal allowed.

  COL-TWO-END marks the end of the second column here.

  COL-THREE-START opens the final column. Desktop publishing brought
  typesetting to personal computers in the nineteen eighties. Page
  description languages such as PostScript described pages as vector
  graphics, and fonts became mathematical outlines rather than physical
  sorts. The craft of spacing and justification survived the transition
  largely unchanged.

  Digital systems then added automated hyphenation, kerning, and
  justification algorithms that rival or exceed hand composition. The
  TeX engine and its descendants remain the reference for mathematical
  and technical documents worldwide.
]
